-- title:
--   Residue at s=1 of a quaternionic adelic zeta integral
-- statement:
--   Let $L/K$ be an extension of number fields with $[L:K]=2$, let $\sigma$ be a $K$-automorphism of $L$ such that every $K$-automorphism of $L$ lies in the subgroup generated by $\sigma$, let $\delta_0 \in \mathrm{GL}_2(L)$, and let $c$ be a unit of $L \otimes_K \mathbb{A}_K$ (the adele ring of $K$). Assume $\delta_0 \cdot \sigma(\delta_0)$ is a scalar matrix $\mathrm{scalar}(z)$ for some $z \in L^\times$, and that $x^{-1}\delta_0\,\sigma(x)$ is a scalar matrix for no $x \in \mathrm{GL}_2(L)$ and $z \in L^\times$; let $v : \mathrm{Fin}\,2 \to L$ be nonzero. Fix a Borel structure on $\mathbb{A}_L$ and an additive Haar measure $\mu_1$ with $\mu_1$ of the adelic box $\{x \mid x_\infty \in \text{infiniteBox}\ L,\ x_{\mathrm{fin}} \in \widehat{\mathcal{O}}_L\}$ equal to $1$; let $\psi$ be an additive character of $\mathbb{A}_L$ which is principal-invariant, continuous and nontrivial, and let $\Phi \in \mathrm{schwartzBruhat2}\ L$, i.e. $\Phi$ lies in the $\mathbb{C}$-span of products $g(x_\infty)h(x_{\mathrm{fin}})$ with $g$ Schwartz on $(\mathrm{Fin}\,2 \to)$ the mixed space of $L$ and $h$ locally constant of compact support on $(\mathrm{Fin}\,2 \to)$ the finite adeles, and assume $\operatorname{Re}\Phi \ge 0$ pointwise. Put $\delta = (\delta_0 \otimes 1)\cdot \mathrm{scalar}(c)$ and let $T' = \{t \in \mathrm{GL}_2(L \otimes_K \mathbb{A}_K) \mid t\,\delta\,(\sigma t)^{-1} = \delta\}$ be the $\sigma$-twisted centralizer of $\delta$, where $\sigma$ acts through the induced automorphism of $\mathrm{GL}_2(L \otimes_K \mathbb{A}_K)$; let $\Gamma'$ be the image in $T'$ of the subgroup $\{t \in \mathrm{GL}_2(L) \mid t\,\delta_0\,(\sigma t)^{-1} = \delta_0\}$ under $l \mapsto l \otimes 1$. Write $E$ for the ring isomorphism $L \otimes_K \mathbb{A}_K \cong \mathbb{A}_K \otimes_K L \cong \mathbb{A}_L$ (the commutation isomorphism followed by [`M4aHerbrand.Bridge.genuineRingEquiv`](def/M4aHerbrand_GenuineTensorEquiv.html#L57)), applied entrywise to matrices, and set $N(t) = \mathrm{ideleNorm}_L(\det E(t))$, the value at $\det E(t)$ of the module of the idele, i.e. of the distributive Haar character of $\mathbb{A}_L$. Let $\tau'$ be a Haar measure on $T'$ that is in addition right invariant, and let $R' \neq \infty$ in $[0,\infty]$ be such that for every subset $D'$ of $T'$ which is a fundamental domain for the right multiplication action of $\Gamma'$ with respect to $\tau'$ and all reals $0 < a \le b$ one has $\tau'(D' \cap \{t \mid N(t) \in [a,b]\}) = R' \cdot \log(b/a)$; let $D_0$ be one such measurable fundamental domain. Finally let $s_1 > 1$ and assume that both $\int_{\{N \ge 1\}} \operatorname{Re}\Phi(E(t)v)\,N(t)^{s_1}\,d\tau'$ and $\int_{\{N \ge 1\}} \lVert (\mathrm{reflectPair}\ \psi\ \mu_1\ \Phi)(E(t)v)\rVert\,d\tau'$ are finite, where $E(t)v$ denotes the matrix $E(t)$ applied to the vector $(\mathrm{algebraMap}\ v_i)_i$ over $\mathbb{A}_L$ and $\mathrm{reflectPair}\ \psi\ \mu_1\ \Phi\,(x) = \widehat{\Phi}(x_1,-x_0)$ for the two-variable adelic Fourier transform with respect to $\psi$ and $\mu_1$. Then, as $s \to 1^+$, $$(s-1)\int_{T'} \operatorname{Re}\Phi(E(t)v)\,N(t)^{s}\,d\tau'(t) \longrightarrow \Big(\int \operatorname{Re}\Phi\,d(\mu_1 \times \mu_1)\Big)\cdot R',$$ all integrals being $[0,\infty]$-valued lower Lebesgue integrals and the product measure on $\mathrm{Fin}\,2 \to \mathbb{A}_L$ being $\mathrm{pairHaar}\ \mu_1$.
--
--   This is the statement that the zeta integral of the quaternion division algebra $D = \{x \in M_2(L) \mid x\delta_0 = \delta_0\sigma(x)\}$ over $K$, formed with a non-negative Schwartz–Bruhat test function and the column vector attached to $v$, has a simple pole at the edge of convergence $s = 1$ with residue the product of the integral of the test function against the self-dual measure and the logarithmic covolume rate $R'$ of the rational units; it is the analogue for $D^\times$ of the Hey–Fujisaki–Weil–Tamagawa residue formula. It feeds the subsequent identification of $R'$ with an explicit product of the discriminant, $\zeta_L(2)$ and the residue of the Dedekind zeta function.
